From: Loong <1031313955@qq.com>
Subject: [PATCH] httpd: stop the "U-Boot FIP" upload field from wiping settings

Reported: upgrading from web recovery 0.1.0 to 0.1.1 by uploading just
preloader.bin and bl31-uboot.fip -- no firmware -- reset every OpenWrt
setting.  The firmware (fit volume) was never touched; what disappeared was
the overlay.

The "U-Boot" field reused the board's own ubi_write_fip script, which does:

  run ubi_remove_rootfs ; ubi check fip && ubi remove fip ; \
      ubi create fip 0x100000 static && ubi write $loadaddr fip $filesize

ubi_remove_rootfs deletes the rootfs_data volume -- the writable overlay
UBIFS mounts on top of the read-only squashfs inside fit, holding every
config change made after first boot.  Next boot's ubi_prepare_rootfs sees
no rootfs_data and creates a fresh empty one, which from OpenWrt's side
looks exactly like a factory reset.

ubi_write_fip is not this session's code and is not being changed: it is
the board's original script (defenvs, predates the web recovery feature),
used by bootmenu entry 4 immediately followed by reset_factory -- a
deliberate full reset, where losing rootfs_data is part of the intent.  The
web page borrowed the same script for what is supposed to be a routine
bootloader swap, which is a different operation with a different meaning
for rootfs_data.  That borrowing, not ubi_write_fip itself, is the bug.

It was never load-bearing in the first place: fip is created at a fixed
0x100000 every time, so an in-place "ubi write" on an already-existing fip
fits inside its own current reservation without freeing anything --
removing then recreating a same-sized volume nets to zero extra PEBs
either way (vmt.c: ubi_remove_volume() returns reserved_pebs to
avail_pebs, ubi_create_volume() then reserves exactly that many back).

Only the very first write, when fip does not exist yet (first migration,
before rootfs_data exists either), needs to create the volume, and only
then is evicting rootfs_data harmless -- so that one case reuses
ubi_write_fip outright (run ubi_write_fip) rather than duplicating its
create-and-evict logic: at that point ubi_write_fip's own "ubi check fip
&& ubi remove fip" is a no-op (nothing to remove), so calling it is exactly
equivalent to what the routine-update branch already established as safe,
just spelled once instead of twice.

httpd_write_fip is a new, independent env script -- same pattern as the
existing httpd_write_bl2 next to mtd_write_bl2 -- so a future change to
either the TFTP-menu reset flow or the web page's routine-update flow
cannot silently change the other's behaviour.

BL2 (preloader.bin) was never implicated: httpd_write_bl2 is a plain
"mtd erase bl2 && mtd write bl2", operating on the bl2 mtd partition
(0x0-0x20000), entirely outside the ubi partition (0x20000 onward) where
every UBI volume, rootfs_data included, lives.  Flashing BL2 alongside FIP
during an upgrade carries no risk to settings.

--- a/defenvs/an7581_nokia_xg-040g-md_env
+++ b/defenvs/an7581_nokia_xg-040g-md_env
@@ -45,6 +45,7 @@
 reset_factory=ubi part ubi ; mw $loadaddr 0x0 0x800 ; ubi write $loadaddr ubootenv 0x800 ; ubi write $loadaddr ubootenv2 0x800
 mtd_write_bl2=mtd erase bl2 && mtd write bl2 $loadaddr
 httpd_write_bl2=mtd erase bl2 && mtd write bl2 $loadaddr 0x800 $filesize
+httpd_write_fip=if ubi check fip ; then ubi write $loadaddr fip $filesize ; else run ubi_write_fip ; fi
 ubi_create_board_data=ubi check bosa || ubi create bosa 0x40000 || run ubi_format ; ubi check ri || ubi create ri 0x40000 || run ubi_format
 ubi_create_env=ubi check ubootenv || ubi create ubootenv 0x1f000 dynamic || run ubi_format ; ubi check ubootenv2 || ubi create ubootenv2 0x1f000 dynamic || run ubi_format
 ubi_format=ubi detach ; mtd erase ubi && ubi part ubi ; reset
